      "first_30_lines": "\"\"\"\npdf_service.py \u2014 PDF Parsing and Chunking Service\n\nThis is the first step in the ingestion pipeline.\nResponsibility: Take a PDF file path \u2192 return text chunks\n                ready for embedding.\n\nPYTHON CONCEPT: What is a \"service\"?\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nA service is a Python module containing functions that do\na specific job. Unlike a router (which handles HTTP), a service\ncontains the BUSINESS LOGIC.\n\nRouters call services. Services don't know about HTTP.\nThis separation makes your code testable and reusable.\n\nThe equivalent in Node.js would be a service class or a\nmodule of helper functions that your route handlers call.\n\nPYTHON CONCEPT: Type Hints for Complex Types\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nPython functions can return complex types:\n    list[dict]        \u2192 a list of dictionaries\n    tuple[int, str]   \u2192 a tuple with int and string\n    dict[str, any]    \u2192 a dict with string keys\n\nWe use these to document what functions return.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport os",
